## Overall Assessment

The paper addresses a genuine and important problem — providing a unified logical characterization of diverse GNN architectures — and the GACNN abstraction and the logical interpretations in Proposition 7 have real value. However, the paper cannot be accepted in its current form. The central result, Theorem 3, is stated without proof or even a proof sketch, and the gap between continuous-representation GNNs and binary-output logical functions is precisely the technically difficult step that prior work (Barceló et al., 2020) had to resolve carefully. The signature claims in Corollary 10 conflate upper bounds with equalities; proving equalities requires lower bounds that the framework as presented does not supply. The definition of "equivalent logic set" uses a finite-approximation relaxation that weakens the "complete" framing substantially, and this relaxation is neither justified nor acknowledged as a limitation. A paper with the title "complete logical framework" and central results of the form "equivalent logic set of arbitrary GNN = Φ" must establish those equivalences rigorously. As submitted, the theoretical core is unvalidated in the main text, and the most important claims rest on appeal to an appendix that cannot be evaluated in this review.
